The Noise-Transmission Burden of Diffusion Network: Why Image Prediction Can Outperform Noise Prediction Skip to main content Report GitHub Issue × Title: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: Description: Why HTML? Report Issue Back to Abstract Download PDF Contents Reading mode Colour theme License: CC BY 4.0 arXiv:2610.34834v1 [stat.ML] 26 Oct 2026 The Noise-Transmission Burden of Diffusion Network: Why Image Prediction Can Outperform Noise Prediction Gyeonghun Kang † † thanks: Equal contribution. Pengxi Liu 1 1 footnotemark: 1 Bingshuo Qian 1 1 footnotemark: 1 Xiang Cheng Affiliation: Duke University, Durham, NC, USA Email: {gyeonghun.kang,pengxi.liu,bingshuo.qian,xiang.cheng}@duke.edu Abstract Clean-image prediction ( x x -prediction) and noise prediction yield equivalent population-optimal denoisers, yet can perform very differently in finite diffusion networks. For noisy input Y t Y_{t} with noise standard deviation σ t \sigma_{t} , a noise predictor f ε , t f_{\varepsilon,t} induces the clean-image estimate X ^ 1 = Y t − σ t ​ f ε , t ​ ( Y t ) \widehat{X}_{1}=Y_{t}-\sigma_{t}f_{\varepsilon,t}(Y_{t}) . This reveals a noise-transmission burden : the predictor must preserve information needed to cancel the input corruption. Under Gaussian corruption of d d -dimensional data, a linear input bottleneck of rank r < d r<d imposes a denoising-error floor σ t 2 ​ ( d − r ) \sigma_{t}^{2}(d-r) even with unrestricted downstream computation, while the corresponding optimal risk for image prediction remains bounded. EDM attenuates this bottleneck contribution at high noise; exact preconditioning cancels it at every noise level. Without an input bottleneck, assuming exact affine recovery of the noisy patch from the model representation, we characterize how the covariance spectrum of corrections beyond linear denoising determines the width required above the patch dimension. ImageNet experiments with JiT show that measured denoising losses reflect the predicted bottleneck floor and its attenuation or cancellation through preconditioning. Further widening yields larger relative denoising gains at low noise, where corrections beyond linear denoising spread across more directions, consistent with our spectral characterization of the required model width. Together, these results provide a denoising-based account of practical generation-quality gaps across prediction targets, preconditioning choices, and model widths. 1 Introduction Why should predicting the clean image be easier than predicting the noise added to it? At the population optimum, image, noise, and velocity predictors are related by simple identities and yield the same denoiser. Yet their performance can differ substantially in finite networks. JiT demonstrates this gap in pixel-space diffusion Transformers: image prediction succeeds with coarse patches and narrow input embeddings in settings where noise or velocity prediction performs poorly ( Li and He, 2025 ) . Understanding this mismatch matters for choosing prediction targets and allocating model capacity. It raises a concrete architectural question: what information must each predictor retain from its input for accurate denoising, and can it preserve that information when an input bottleneck reduces the representation dimension below the patch dimension? Prior work shows that both data geometry and architecture influence prediction-target preference ( Li and He, 2025 ; Jin and Wang, 2026 ; Gagneux et al., 2026 ) , but does not quantify how architectural constraints on retained input information affect denoising. Existing output-rank bounds establish a loss floor when predictions are confined to a restricted output subspace ( Zheng et al., 2025 ) . A complementary limitation arises when the architecture discards noisy-input information: even an unrestricted decoder may then lack the information needed for accurate denoising. How does this error depend on the input bottleneck and noise level, even with unrestricted downstream computation, and how does an explicit input path change it? Once input compression is removed, how much additional width is needed to retain the noisy input while representing corrections beyond linear denoising? Appendix C reviews existing results and their relation to these questions. We address these questions through the clean-image estimate induced by a noise predictor. For a clean image X 1 ∈ ℝ d X_{1}\in\mathbb{R}^{d} and normalized Gaussian-corrupted observation Y t = X 1 + σ t ​ ε Y_{t}=X_{1}+\sigma_{t}\varepsilon , the estimate is X ^ 1 = Y t − σ t ​ f ε , t ​ ( Y t ) \widehat{X}_{1}=Y_{t}-\sigma_{t}f_{\varepsilon,t}(Y_{t}) . The explicit input term carries the corruption, and the learned branch must supply the noise contribution needed to cancel it. We call the requirement to retain this information a noise-transmission burden . This viewpoint connects input compression, output preconditioning, and hidden width through their effects on attainable denoising accuracy. Based on this viewpoint, our theoretical analysis and ImageNet experiments with JiT yield three linked results. 1. An input bottleneck imposes a denoising-error floor for noise prediction that grows quadratically with noise. Theorem 1 and Corollary 2 establish the floor σ t 2 ​ ( d − r ) \sigma_{t}^{2}(d-r) when the architecture imposes a linear input bottleneck of rank r < d r<d , even with unrestricted downstream computation. The result requires only a finite second moment for the clean data and no manifold assumption; the corresponding optimal risk for image prediction remains bounded under the same bottleneck. Experiments show denoising loss approaching the floor at high noise (Figure 2 b), with affine noise-recovery probes below the ceiling (Figure 3 ; Table 3 ). 2. EDM alleviates the noise-transmission burden at high noise; exact preconditioning cancels it. Theorem 3 and Corollary 4 characterize how an explicit input path changes the denoising-error floor imposed by the input bottleneck. EDM suppresses it at high noise, while exact preconditioning removes it at every noise level. Consistent with this analysis, EDM improves FID from 412.11 412.11 for noise prediction to 52.28 52.28 , and exact preconditioning reaches 9.15 9.15 , close to image prediction’s 9.13 9.13 (Figure 2 a). Concurrently, EDM’s denoising loss approaches that of image prediction at high noise, exact preconditioning tracks image prediction across noise levels (Figures 2 b and 7 ), and learned skip coefficients closely track their exact-preconditioning values at moderate and high noise (Figure 6 a). 3. Without an input bottleneck, corrections beyond linear denoising determine how far model width must exceed the patch dimension. Assuming the noisy input patch can be recovered exactly by an affine readout of the model representation, Theorem 5 and Corollary 6 characterize the minimum representation width needed for a chosen level of denoising accuracy. Since such a representation already supports linear denoising, the additional width needed depends on the remaining nonlinear correction. Both the correction’s magnitude and the distribution of its variance across directions matter. In our experiments, modest widening improves generation, denoising, and affine noisy-input recovery (Figures 4 a–b, 5 , and 9 ). Further widening yields larger relative denoising gains at low noise, where the required nonlinear corrections are spread across more directions than at high noise (Figures 4 b–c and 8 b–c). 2 Problem setup Let X 1 ∈ ℝ d X_{1}\in\mathbb{R}^{d} be a vectorized clean image drawn from a fixed distribution with 𝔼 ​ ‖ X 1 ‖ 2 < ∞ \mathbb{E}\|X_{1}\|^{2}<\infty . The variable t ∈ [ 0 , 1 ] t\in[0,1] indexes a prescribed corruption path. We study a fixed interior time 0 < t < 1 0<t<1 , at which the signal and noise coefficients are both positive, and define the corrupted image and normalized observation by X t = a t ​ X 1 + b t ​ ε , Y t := X t / a t = X 1 + σ t ​ ε 